WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.020
Speaker 1: It is great to welcome to the program
today. Andrew Sullivan, who's a blogger at

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:11.459
The Weekly Dish. His book, now out in paperback,
is out on a limb collected writing from 1989

00:00:11.459 --> 00:00:17.450
to 2021. You know, Andrew, it's so great to
have you on because as a progressive, my very

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:24.030
left wing audience will say who on the right
is interesting to listen to because I'm not

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:30.410
getting what I need intellectually from Matt
Gaetz, for example. And I will often say,

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.559
you know, I actually think Andrew Sullivan
writes a lot of really interesting things.

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:40.289
But the strange thing is I see very little
in common at this point between you and sort

00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:45.000
of where the American Republican Party is.
I mean, do you feel like you still see your

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.789
politics in that party?
Speaker 6: I've never seen my politics in

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:59.489
that party. I never identified as a Republican,
have been pretty hostile to successive Republican

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:06.360
presidents. I mean, Bush and Trump, for example,
a big supporter of Obama. No, my my view is

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:15.740
really from a more English conservative position
of limited government, of free society, skepticism

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:23.910
about grand changes to society in general,
and a belief in the West and its core values.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:29.740
And and that meant that I did not have a huge
problem, for example, in supporting Barack

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.610
Obama, whom I regarded as a bit of a moderate,
a bit of an old school, actually. I always

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:41.600
thought of him as a 1970s kind of Republican.
Mm hmm. And so I. I don't fit in, is the truth.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:50.640
But I am definitely not a lefty, and I'm definitely
not a liberal in the progressive sense. I'm

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.480
a skeptic of those those traditions.
Speaker 1: When you look at to pick something

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:03.390
specific, the increased willingness from the
modern American right wing to use government

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:10.110
power to achieve whatever it is they're looking
to achieve, rather than stepping back into

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:17.290
what might be called small government conservatism,
doing the absolute least. Does that concern

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:22.451
you above and beyond just, you know, whether
you identify with the movement or not, because

00:02:22.451 --> 00:02:28.470
it's antithetical to the way you believe government
should operate? Or is it is it not as broad

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.920
a concern as some more traditional conservatives
point out?

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.780
Speaker 6: I think that at some level, you
know, the right in terms of the culture war

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:44.050
particularly does have a right to self-defense.
I mean, I don't think that's completely outrageous.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:49.290
I don't think it's outrageous, for example,
that we should have states decide what their

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:56.300
abortion regime is going to be through democratic
processes. I, i, I don't think it's crazy

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:03.540
for a state, for example, to look at its own
educational curricula and positions. And if

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.780
it's that has been altered dramatically in
ways that the the politics of the state doesn't

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:14.569
quite accept, then I don't think it's out
of bounds for a governor or school boards

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:21.680
to do within the classic Democratic tradition
a change of policy or a reversal of policy.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:28.000
But I do think there's a sense of using the
state as a wielding a sort of a weapon to

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.140
punish people. So, for example, let me give
you a to Sanchez is perfectly within his rights

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:44.050
to say no in in Florida we are going to through
the legislature, change curriculum and and

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:50.489
pass this this law. It's another thing for
him then to use the power of the governor

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:55.620
to punish a company, Disney, for disagreeing
with him. So there's a distinction there.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:00.349
And I think sometimes it can get muddled up.
I don't think the right has to constantly

00:04:00.349 --> 00:04:06.629
disarm, but I do think the right has lost
a sense of the limits of government, the need

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:13.240
to let society evolve on its own and to be
tolerant of other points of view and allow

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:18.470
them their place in the sun. And that that
goes for both sides, by the way. And I think

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:25.100
a great deal of the pushback from the right
on some of these culture issues is a response

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:29.350
to aggressive acts by the progressive left.
Speaker 1: When it comes to Democratically

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:35.020
deciding abortion law at the state level.
Like you mentioned, Kansas had an actual vote

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:41.120
of the people of the population. Other states
like Indiana, it's legislators who got together

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.220
and said we're banning it after. I think six
weeks is the latest proposal, with very, very

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:51.050
few exceptions. One could argue that that's
democratic in the sense that it's legislators

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:56.490
elected by the people who are deciding that.
But it runs counter to public opinion, as

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:01.270
best we can tell at this point in time. So
are both of. Those examples of democratically

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.770
deciding or is only when the people vote an
example of that?

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:09.400
Speaker 6: Well, I think the Kansas thing
was a very particular question because they

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:17.130
had already had some sort of decision barring
made some kind of vote. Yes, there's a referendum

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:22.380
inevitable. No, no. I don't think it really
matters how that democracy is pursued. It

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.720
may be that in the short term, for example,
there are lots of people in Republicans elected

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.040
at state legislatures who are more to the
right on abortion than most of their voters

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:40.360
are. Absolute will vote them out or or let
your views be known to them to make sure they

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:45.550
are not following some preexisting ideological
commitment, which, you know, for many years

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:50.860
it was so easy for Republicans to say, I'm
for banning all abortion, period. And they

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.860
were never called on it because no one was
ever going to be enforcing it. Now that it's

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:01.400
having to be enforced, I think you're going
to get actual, real compromises and decisions

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:07.009
that are made state by state. And I think
liberals or pro-life choices in general are

00:06:07.009 --> 00:06:12.050
way too pessimistic about their chances in
that process and I think will probably end

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:18.520
up in the US pretty not that far away for
many countries in Western Europe. And although

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:23.320
we will have some outliers on the conservative
front, but you know, America is more conservative

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:29.490
than parts of Europe and I, I don't have a
problem with states coming to different positions

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:32.830
on questions like that.
Speaker 1: The other because we're talking

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:37.850
about a lot of cultural and social issues,
it was notable to me I've sort of seen this

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:42.610
building and look in watching Trump rallies
over the last year or two, 18 months. But

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:50.100
it was very notable at CPAC that the biggest
applause lines were the more overtly homophobic

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:56.580
and transphobic lines, jokes about pronouns.
You know, I don't have the full list in front

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:01.389
of me, but when you saw the crowd really get
excited, they were dead. When Trump was talking

00:07:01.389 --> 00:07:05.620
about himself as the biggest victim in the
history of the western of Western civilization.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:11.570
That did not excite the crowd too much, but
the stuff about trans swimmers and Ted Cruz's

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:18.060
pronouns and it really excited the crowd.
Do you agree that there's been a sort of resurgence

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:23.150
of that sentiment in the modern American right,
and are you concerned about it?

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:28.669
Speaker 6: I think I am within some aspects
of the conservative, right. I mean, we're

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:35.530
talking about the Texas CPAC. Yes. We are
talking about the pretty hardcore fanatics.

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:42.500
And they are so polarized that anything pro-gay,
for example, gets attacked. But when you actually

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:48.180
look at the polling data of regular Republicans,
you now see a majority of Republicans favoring

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:55.139
marriage equality. What I do think you're
seeing is an understandable response to a

00:07:55.139 --> 00:08:04.930
very aggressive push by essentially gender,
queer and gender critical theorists to redefine

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:10.550
what sex is, to redefine what gender is, and
to teach those three definitions to 3 to 4

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:15.460
year olds without their parents even being
informed. And I think that has led to a backlash.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:20.240
I honestly think it's understandable. It's
led to a backlash. And there are plenty of

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:26.940
us who are very supportive of gay and trans
rights, who are not nonetheless wedded to

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:32.560
this rather post-modern understanding that
biological sex is basically nonexistent, that

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.099
you pick and choose whichever gender you have,
and that there are more than 55 different

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:41.539
genders to pick from, and that all of them
trump sex in every particular instance. I

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:45.900
don't buy any of that. And I think insofar
as there's been resistance to it, it's been

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:52.140
a function of the left overreaching rather
than the right demonstrating its its intolerance.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:55.450
That's not to say I defend some of this stuff.
Speaker 1: Well, I mean, you know, through

00:08:55.450 --> 00:09:01.670
some of the some of the things you just said,
I'm also against I've just not seen them being

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:06.460
taught to four year olds anywhere. I mean,
where and I'm not saying this rhetorically,

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:12.160
like where are they teaching four year olds
that there is no biological basis at all for

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:17.010
gender and that there's 55 choices to be.
I just haven't seen that.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:21.980
Speaker 6: Well, I recommend you check a couple
of pieces I've done recently at the Weekly

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:28.980
Dish on this state. Curricula are now mandating
places that were as in Florida, as in New

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:36.160
Jersey and other places with that gender identity
and homosexual and sexual orientation be taught

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.680
from kindergarten onwards. Now, how that is
translated is a different question, but I

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:49.200
can also show you plenty of children's books,
plenty of lesson plans in which this is argued

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.230
for as early as three or four. And what they
say, of course, they're not using words like

00:09:54.230 --> 00:10:00.240
biological sex or they're saying and this
is the phrase that comes across. Quite starkly

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:05.870
to me. You this is this is to a child who
is kindergartner. You can be a boy or a girl

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:14.050
or both or neither or something else entirely.
And it's up to you to choose. Well, I don't

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:17.640
I honestly, I think telling three year olds
that before they've even wrap their heads

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:24.831
around boy and girl seems to me outrageously
over the top and certainly not something a

00:10:24.831 --> 00:10:30.290
lot of gay people even think should be taught
and certainly affects gay kids who themselves

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:37.380
have a slightly different perspective, which
is that, yes, we're boys, but we just happen

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.720
to be attracted to other boys. Now, they may
not be they will not be aware of that in any

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:45.630
real sense at three or four or five. I don't
see it as a problem with restricting it to

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:49.990
kids who are eight years old or above. And
I don't think that's some sort of fascist

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:55.700
move. I think that I think the ideologues
got out of control and they're being slightly

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:58.240
reined back in. And I don't think that's the
end of the world.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:03.130
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, it's hard for me
to respond substantively because I just haven't

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:06.450
seen what you're describing. So I'll I'll
do some research on that.

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:12.810
Speaker 6: I think as books, yes. They're
really the ways in which you translate it

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:18.490
to children. And I think it can be rather
disorienting for children to be taught this.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:24.399
Speaker 1: That young as a political question,
what would you like to see the American right,

00:11:24.399 --> 00:11:28.640
whether it's through the Republican Party
or outside of it, wherever you follow it.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:34.399
What should be the substance of the platforms
on which those candidates run? Because especially

00:11:34.399 --> 00:11:40.100
in the Trump era, they don't even really pretend
to be about economic policy. They'll vaguely

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:46.230
talk about taxes and regulation, but there's
very little substance there. They're like,

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:51.000
What substance is it? This stuff, the stuff
about what four year olds are taught, because

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:57.140
that seems sort of flimsy as well as a platform.
Speaker 6: It is flimsy as a platform. But

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:03.510
I do think there is a potent policy mix that's
on the table, and that is for the Republican

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:09.200
Party to go left on economics, to do things
like supporting, like take the pro-life position

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:14.470
if they actually got their act together and
decided to really fund health care for pregnant

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:21.240
women, that they actually did put some beef
behind support for bringing children into

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:26.490
the world along with more restrictions on
abortion, then I think they would have much

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:34.700
a fairer hearing at least. And I think moving
left on economics in terms of trade, I think

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:40.880
moving right on immigration and that combination
with a certain amount of cultural not right

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:45.530
wing or but conservatism in the sense that
we don't really want to redefine gender. We

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:51.380
don't want to suddenly decide that everyone
has to overcome whiteness in order to become

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:56.370
a fully functioning member of society, those
kinds of things. I think that's a very potent

00:12:56.370 --> 00:13:02.810
platform. It's potent in Europe and it would
be potent here if it hadn't been consumed

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:09.769
by all this awful authoritarian instincts,
by the cult worship of Trump, by the anti

00:13:09.769 --> 00:13:18.251
institutional, anti-conservative radicalism
of many of these groups. And I think there

00:13:18.251 --> 00:13:24.420
is a center there, a center right that will
be made anew in the new world around nationalism,

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:30.840
around trade, around immigration. But that
could be sane and would work very well. And

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:35.240
unfortunately, I think the Republican Party
seems currently incapable of coming up with

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:37.130
that.
Speaker 1: Is there anybody right now that

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:41.630
embodies that? I mean, who when you I know
you don't like Trump. My guess is you're not

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:47.800
big on DeSantis. Are there people currently
in politics in the U.S. that match this ideology

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:49.620
that you're talking about?
Speaker 2: Well, look.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:55.399
Speaker 6: To Sanchez has a certain edge to
him. And he's he's posing as Trump essentially

00:13:55.399 --> 00:14:00.240
at this point. Yes. But when you look at his
early time in Florida, when you look at, for

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:06.050
example, he's been quite sensible and pragmatic
on climate change in terms of resilience in

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:11.940
Florida, has been doing quite a lot of sane
stuff on the environment. I think if you could

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:17.149
get past the the personality cult of someone
like Trump, and I think if you could start

00:14:17.149 --> 00:14:21.279
delivering. So, for example, if Trump had
actually delivered on his wall, even though

00:14:21.279 --> 00:14:26.580
we didn't, I do think you could have a more
successful presidency than he did with Trump.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:33.620
But Trump is just sui generis. He is a crazy
person. He has attacked this country's institutions

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:39.269
and its democracy. He should not be anywhere
near political office. So from my point of

00:14:39.269 --> 00:14:45.709
view, unless that is resolved, we can't support
I can't support anything Republican. But if

00:14:45.709 --> 00:14:51.600
a Republican were to emerge who would actually
acknowledge and recognize our constitutional

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:59.070
order. He can reform the institutions, but
not destroy them and create a policy agenda

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:04.920
that. Is more left economically and more right
socially then. That's a perfectly decent outcome

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:09.170
for the future of the right in America. The
question is who can do it? I think, to be

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:13.250
honest with you, I have an open mind about
whether DeSantis is that person or whether

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:19.040
he is not that person. I will see.
Speaker 1: People like Mitt Romney and Liz

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:26.390
Cheney. How what's your perspective on them?
Speaker 6: I. I. I have what we call a newfound

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:31.180
respect for Liz Cheney. I do think that what
she's done has required courage and integrity.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:38.120
And I think she's right. I really do. I think
Mitt Romney's move on supporting family life

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:46.510
and the the economic stresses of young families
with kids who are struggling to get by is

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:53.180
also very positive. I hope it seems to me
that until we get rid of Trump, someone like

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:58.779
Liz Cheney is not going to be a player. But
she's got a long life and we don't know what

00:15:58.779 --> 00:16:03.769
happens. But I'm I'm very happy. I don't know
about you. But when I look back at my worry

00:16:03.769 --> 00:16:10.180
about Romney in 2012, I'm like, Jesus, give
me any day of the week. Now, he would be far

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:16.720
preferable to these these nut balls. And he's
he's a good guy. You know, at some point,

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:22.170
just someone who respects the rule of law
and the Constitution and says so publicly

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:26.460
is such a relief on the Republican side.
Speaker 1: No, I know what you mean. I recently

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:33.240
rewatched this Netflix documentary about Romney's
I believe it was the 2012 run and without,

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:39.920
you know, rehabilitating his policies specifically,
which I preferred Obama at the time. But it's

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:46.210
hard to feel like it would have been a crisis
if Mitt Romney had won in 2012 in the way

00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:51.870
that we've seen in the last four years, because
he seems so comparatively benign and inoffensive.

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:56.730
Speaker 6: There are two things here. You
can disagree with someone's policies. Yeah,

00:16:56.730 --> 00:17:00.970
that's what democracy is. Right. Right. Vote
for the other guy. But when you lose trust

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:06.259
in that person's respect for the system, for
the institutions, and for the rule of law,

00:17:06.259 --> 00:17:11.100
it's something else entirely. We. Since Trump
became the nominee, we've been a different

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:16.769
world. We have been in a Democratic emergency
because this is someone at the center of our

00:17:16.769 --> 00:17:22.019
system who despises our system and who will
seek to undermine it doesn't even understand

00:17:22.019 --> 00:17:28.350
it. So putting that aside, it would be lovely
to get back to a really good debate about

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:32.249
policy. Right. How do we best protect the
environment? You know, I mean, and the current

00:17:32.249 --> 00:17:37.179
bill, for example, is a bit of a hodgepodge,
isn't it? But it's a decent hodgepodge. It's

00:17:37.179 --> 00:17:43.021
a compromise that move things forward. And
I the same with abortion in the States. Even

00:17:43.021 --> 00:17:48.460
though you may hate the idea it's being done,
it's still a process, a political process.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:54.299
Find a compromise, move things forward in
a way. And that's all liberal democracy should

00:17:54.299 --> 00:17:59.350
be about. We're not about solving every human
problem. We're about making things a bit better

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:06.330
if we can. And I want to kind of dial back
these existential claims about what politics

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:15.690
can do and get back to the difficult, unsatisfying,
but absolutely. The normally important compromising,

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:22.480
legislating. And I think the current Congress,
after a rough start, has begun to hit its

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:27.619
stride on that. I agree. I will be I'll be
very interested to see how the public responds.

00:18:27.619 --> 00:18:33.049
Yeah, because I think the public wants this.
I really do. Even though it won't solve everything,

00:18:33.049 --> 00:18:37.960
it shows that we can do something. And I hope
I hope that's rewarded.

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:43.239
Speaker 1: Yeah. And some of the polling data
over the last two months suggests that things

00:18:43.239 --> 00:18:47.880
are looking different for a midterm than than
they would historically be expected to look

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:53.470
at. We'll have to wait two and a half months
now and see. The book now out in paperback

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:59.309
is out on a limb collected writing from 89
to 2021. We've been speaking with blogger

00:18:59.309 --> 00:19:03.369
Andrew Sullivan, whose work you can read at
The Weekly Dish. So great having you on.

00:19:03.369 --> 00:19:08.629
Speaker 6: Thanks so much for having this
